{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Then Moses sang, and the children of Israel.</b> It is in the present tense. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said: it does not say שר but ישיר — from here is a hint to the resurrection of the dead from the Torah." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> 'אז ישיר משה' — there is אז for the past and there is אז for the future. Past: (Genesis 4:26) אז הוחל; (Exodus 4:26) אז אמרה; אז ישיר משה; (Numbers 21:17) אז ישיר ישראל; (Joshua 10:12) אז ידבר יהושע; (I Chronicles 15:2) אז אמר דוד; (I Kings 8:12) אז אמר שלמה. And for the future: (Isaiah 60:5) אז תראי ונהרת; there (ibid. 58) אז יבקע כשחר אורך; there (ibid. 35) אז ידלג כאיל; (Jeremiah 31:13) אז תשמח בתולה; (Psalms 126:2) אז ימלא שחוק פינו; אז יאמרו בגוים — behold, these are future. Moses said: with אז I called out 'merchant' before the Holy One, blessed be He, and 'from when (מאז) I came to Pharaoh to speak in Your name' — in the very expression with which I offended, I praise Him — praise to the One who is above seven firmaments. Israel said: when the Holy One, blessed be He, exacted retribution from the generation of Enosh, over whom the waters of Ocean rose and flooded a third of the world, as it says (Genesis 4:26) אז הוחל לקרא — and we, for whom He made a way through the waters and took vengeance on the Egyptians — with אז we praise Him. And the reason it mentioned 'Moses and the children of Israel, saying,' is to teach that Moses and the children of Israel merited to recite that praise themselves, and that song which praises the attribute called 'זאת לה' — this is the bride who praises the King and says שיר השירים אשר לשלמה — and understand this. And in the Song of the Well he alludes to this, with the help of the blessed Name." "<b>Saying.</b> The doubling of 'saying' is because they doubled the language of the song, for all the verses of the song and all its themes are doubled; and so you find in Hallel, from (Psalms 118:5) מן המצר קראתי יה ענני במרחב יה, until (ibid. 24) זה היום עשה ה' — the entire language is doubled. And the doubled praise hints at a power within a power; therefore the song comes in doubled language, for it is praise to the attribute of mercy and to the Shekhinah — אשירה לה' — and afterward עזי וזמרת יה, ה' שמו — and afterward ימינך, which is the attribute of judgment: מי כמכה באלים ה' — and afterward מי כמוכה נאדר בקדש, which is 'adorned in His holy habitation' in heaven, for among all the supernal beings none resembles His glory, for He is 'adorned in His holy habitation' — מכון לשבתך פעלת ה' — and afterward מקדש ה' אל\"ף דל\"ת — and understand this. Or one may say: ויאמרו לאמר means with movement of the lips, as in (Exodus 17:4) ויצעק משה אל ה' לאמר — that one who prays must articulate with his lips. And I found support for this from what they said in the Midrash: ויאמרו לאמר — Rabbi Nehemiah says: the holy spirit rested upon Israel and they said the song like people reciting the Shema." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> 'And the suckling babes said, saying' — the babes at the breast; even the fetuses in their mothers' wombs. This is what they expounded: How did Israel say the song? A suckling lying on its mother's lap, and a nursing child at its mother's breast — when they saw the Shekhinah, the suckling raised its neck and the nursing child released the breast from its mouth, and they said: זה אלי ואנוהו, as it says (Psalms 8:3) מפי עוללים ויונקים וגו'. And even the fetuses in their mothers' wombs said the song at the Sea, as it says (Psalms 68:27) במקהלות ברכו אלהים ה' ממקור ישראל." "<b>For He is highly exalted.</b> Since among all the beasts none is as proud as the horse, and the rider is proud upon it, Scripture explained that the Holy One, blessed be He, is exalted above them both." "<b>And by way of the Midrash.</b> ״כי גאה גאה״ — there are four that are proud (גאים): the lion among beasts, the ox among domestic animals, the eagle among birds, and man over all of them, as it is said (Genesis 1:28): ״ורדו בדגת הים ובעוף השמים״ and so forth. What did the Holy One, blessed be He, do? He set the likeness of all these proud ones upon His Throne of Glory, as it is said (Ezekiel 1:10): ״ודמות פניהם פני אדם ופני אריה אל הימין לארבעתם ופני שור מהשמאל לארבעתם ופני נשר לארבעתם.״ Said the Holy One, blessed be He: since they are proud, I am proud over them, as it is said: ״כי גאה גאה״ — behold, He is proud over all the proud ones. End quote. And this is what is written (Psalms 113:4): ״רם על כל גוים ה׳״ — read it ״גוים״ as ״גאים״ (the proud), and this is He who is proud over the holy beasts." "<b>And further</b> they said in the Midrash: Another interpretation — <b>כי גאה גאה</b>: He displayed pride over all who act proudly; from all who act proudly before Him, in the very thing in which they act proudly He exacts punishment from them. <b>Egypt and Pharaoh</b> grew proud through water and were struck through water. <b>The generation of the Flood</b> grew proud through water — they said, 'We have no need for the drop of rain; rather, ״ואד יעלה מן הארץ״' — so He exacted punishment from them through the waters of the Flood, as it is said (Genesis 7:11): ״ביום הזה נבקעו כל מעינות תהום רבה״ — ״תהום עליון״ (the upper deep) and ״תהום תחתון״ (the lower deep). <b>The men of Sodom</b> grew proud in their wealth, in the abundance of silver and gold, and sought to eliminate the feet of wayfarers so they would not need to give them anything, and the Holy One, blessed be He, eliminated them from the world; as the verse says (Job 28:4): ״פרץ נחל מעם גר, הנשכחים מני רגל דלו מאנוש נעו,״ and it is written (Job 28:12): ״לפיד בוז לעשתות שאנן נכון למועדי רגל,״ and it is written there: ״ישליו אהלים לשודדים ובטוחות למרגיזי אל,״ and it is written (Ezekiel 16:49): ״הנה זה היה עון סדום אחותך גאון שבעת לחם ושלות השקט היה לה ולבנותיה ויד עני ואביון לא החזיקה.״ <b>Pharaoh</b> grew proud in his chariots, as it is said: ״ויקח שש מאות רכב בחור״ — He exacted punishment from him through chariots, as it is said: ״מרכבות פרעה וחילו״ and so forth. <b>Sisera</b> grew proud in his chariot, as it is said (Judges 4:13): ״ויזעק סיסרא את כל רכבו״ — He exacted punishment from him through his chariot, as it is said (Judges 5:20): ״הכוכבים ממסלותם נלחמו עם סיסרא.״ <b>Samson</b> — in that which he grew proud, from it He exacted punishment from him. He grew proud in his eyes, as it is said (Judges 14:3): ״אותה קח לי כי היא ישרה בעיני״ — He exacted punishment from him through his eyes, as it is said (Judges 16:21): ״ויאחזוהו פלשתים וינקרו את עיניו.״ And just as he sinned in Gaza, as it is said (Judges 16:1): ״וילך שמשון עזתה וירא שם אשה,״ so He exacted punishment from him in Gaza, as it is said (Judges 16:21): ״ויורידו אותו עזתה ויאסרוהו בנחושתים.״ <b>Absalom</b> boasted in his hair and was struck through his hair, as it is said (2 Samuel 18:9): ״ויחזק ראשו באלה.״ <b>Sennacherib</b> grew proud in his chariot, as it is said (Isaiah 37:24): ״ברוב רוכבי אני עליתי מרום הרים ירכתי לבנון״ — what is written? (2 Kings 19:35): ״ויצא מלאך ה׳ ויך במחנה אשור.״ <b>Nebuchadnezzar</b> grew proud and said (Isaiah 14:14): ״אעלה על במתי עב״ and so forth. What is written there? ״אך אל שאול תורד״ and so forth. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: You separated yourself from mankind to make yourself a god — I will separate you from mankind to have your dwelling with the beasts; that is what is written (Daniel 4:29–30): ״ולך טרדין מן אנשא ועם חיות ברא להוה מדורך.״ Therefore it is written: ״גאה גאה.״" "<b>The horse and his rider He hurled into the sea.</b> For they were all before Him like one horse and one rider; and thus it is written (Deuteronomy 20:1): ״כי תצא למלחמה על אויביך וראית סוס ורכב״ — they are all before Him like one horse and one rider." "<b>The horse and his rider.</b> The horse is bound to the rider and the rider is bound to the horse; they rise to the heights and descend to the depths and are not separated one from the other. Therefore it is said ״רמה בים״ — corresponding to what rises to the heights; and therefore it is said ״ירה בים״ — corresponding to what descends to the depths. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted: <b>סוס ורוכבו רמה בים</b> — the rider would say to the horse: 'Yesterday I was pulling you to the Nile and you would not come after me, and now you are pulling me into the sea!' And the horse would answer him: ״רמה בים״ — see what is in the sea! The height of the world I see in the sea."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {2 "The text 'אדיר במעון קדשו' appears to cite a known liturgical phrase (cf. the Shabbat song 'אדיר אדירנו'); the phrase 'אדיר במעון' is translated in that sense. The parenthetical reference '(שם סח)' in comment 3 is normalized to Psalm 68.", 3 "The verse from Psalms 68 is numbered 68:27 following the Hebrew verse numbering (verse 26 in some English systems).", 7 "Rabbeinu Bahya cites Deuteronomy 20:1 where the singular forms סוס ורכב ('horse and chariot') are read as though the entire enemy force appeared as one; the midrashic reading underlying this comment construes the singular as a collective."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:59:53.766851194Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}